# Reducing Perovskite/C60 Interface Losses via Sequential Interface Engineering for Efficient Perovskite/Silicon Tandem Solar Cell

## abstract
Wide-bandgap (WBG) perovskite solar cells hold tremendous potential for realizing efficient tandem solar cells. However, nonradiative recombination and carrier transport losses occurring at the perovskite/electron-selective contact (e.g. C60 ) interface present significant obstacles in approaching their theoretical efficiency limit. To address this, a sequential interface engineering (SIE) strategy that involves the deposition of ethylenediamine diiodide (EDAI2 ) followed by sequential deposition of 4-Fluoro-Phenethylammonium chloride (4F-PEACl) is implemented. The SIE technique synergistically narrows the conduction band offset and reduces recombination velocity at the perovskite/C60 interface. The best-performing WBG perovskite solar cell (1.67 eV) delivers a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 21.8% and an impressive open-circuit voltage of 1.262 V. Moreover, through integration with double-textured silicon featuring submicrometer pyramid structures, a stabilized PCE of 29.6% is attained for a 1 cm2 monolithic perovskite/silicon tandem cell (certified PCE of 29.0%).

Reducing Perovskite/ $\textcircled{6}$ Interface Losses via Sequential Interface Engineering for Efficient Perovskite/Silicon Tandem Solar Cell
Wide-bandgap (WBG) perovskite solar cells hold tremendous potential for realizing efficient tandem solar cells. However, nonradiative recombination and carrier transport losses occurring at the perovskite/electron-selective contact (e.g. $\mathsf{\pmb{C}}_{60}\mathsf{\Pi}_{}^{})$ interface present significant obstacles in approaching their theoretical efficiency limit. To address this, a sequential interface engineering (SIE) strategy that involves the deposition of ethylenediamine diiodide $(\mathsf{E D A l}_{2})$ followed by sequential deposition of 4-Fluoro-Phenethylammonium chloride (4F-PEACl) is implemented. The SIE technique synergistically narrows the conduction band offset and reduces recombination velocity at the perovskite $1C_{60}$ interface. The best-performing WBG perovskite solar cell (1.67 eV) delivers a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $21.8\%$ and an impressive open-circuit voltage of 1.262 V. Moreover, through integration with double-textured silicon featuring submicrometer pyramid structures, a stabilized PCE of $29.6\%$ is attained for a $\mathsf{1c m}^{2}$ monolithic perovskite/silicon tandem cell (certified PCE of $29.0\%$ .
## 1. Introduction
Monolithic perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells have emerged as a promising avenue to achieve exceptionally efficient photovoltaic devices, with theoretical efficiencies surpassing $43\%$ .[ 1 ] Demonstrating this potential, reported power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) have soared as high as $33.9\%$ for $\mathrm{~1~}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ illuminated areas this year.[2] However, the practical implementation of widebandgap (WBG) perovskite top cells with an optimal bandgap of $1.65{-}1.70\ \mathrm{eV}$ still faces substantial challenges arising from severe nonradiative recombination and carrier transport losses. The unfavorable energy-level alignment and high defect density at the interface between the perovskite absorber and the electron-selective contact $\left(\mathrm{C}_{60}\right)$ are particularly accentuated in WBG perovskite cells when compared to normal-bandgap counterparts,[3–6] resulting in extra open-circuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ and fill factor (FF) deficits.[7–9]
Extensive research efforts to address these challenges have focused on passivation strategies, primarily relying on onestep methods. This involves introducing monomeric large alkylammonium species or inserting mild dipole layers between the transport layers and perovskite. Commonly used large alkylammonium passivators, including oleylammonium iodide (OLAI), phenylethylammonium iodide (PEAI), 1,4- butanediammonium iodide (BDAI), and guanidinium iodide (GAI) forms a 2D/3D perovskite heterojunction that adjusts the surface energy levels and passivates surface defects.[10–12] Despite their efficacy, the presence of significant $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ -induced recombination losses in the initial monolayer of $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ , still limits the overall passivation efficiency.[6] Additionally, strategies involving the insertion of dipole layers such as LiF or spacers interfaces such as MgFx, these inserted layers improved charge transport and extraction between fullerene and perovskite.[13] However, when the interlayer thickness exceeds $1\mathrm{nm}$ , they can adversely impede the charge transport, making the deposition process require precise control.[14]
To overcome these limitations, we present a novel and highly effective dual-step passivation approach involving ethylenediamine diiodide $(\mathrm{EDAI}_{2})$ and sequential 4-FluoroPhenethylammonium chloride (4F-PEACl) deposition. $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ serves as a modifying layer to narrow the conduction band offset between the perovskite and $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ layers, while 4F-PEACl acts as a positive dipole layer to further reduce recombination losses. This sequential post-treatment synergistically eliminated the non-radiative loss between the perovskite and $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ interfaces without compromising charge extraction. We achieved a champion PCE of $21.8\%$ with an impressive $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.262\mathrm{V}$ in $1.67–\mathrm{eV}$ WBG PSCs. By integrating with double-textured Si featuring submicrometer pyramid structures, we demonstrated a stabilized PCE of $29.6\%$ for a $\approx1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ monolithic perovskite-silicon tandem solar cell (certified quasi-steady-state efficiency of $29.0\%$ with $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of 1.946 V).
## 2. Results and Discussion
The WBG perovskite studied in our manuscript is $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{FA}_{0.8}\mathrm{MA}_{0.15}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.75}\mathrm{Br}_{0.25})_{3}$ , the bandgap of the WBG perovskite is $1.67\ \mathrm{eV}$ calculated from the tauc plot in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). The device configuration of WBG perovskite solar cells was depicted in Figure 1a, where a NiO/SAM (self-assembly monolayer) hole transport bilayer was utilized according to our previous report.[15] To investigate the interfacial recombination at the perovskite $/\mathrm{C}_{60}$ interface, a single-deposition of ethylenediamine diiodide $\mathrm{(EDAI_{2})}$ ), 4-FluoroPhenethylammonium chloride (4F-PEACl), and their sequential combination were employed. The post-treated films were labeled as $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ , 4F-PEACl, and sequential interface engineering (SIE), while the untreated films were denoted as control samples. As shown in Figure 1b, all the morphology of the perovskite films remained unchanged after post-treatment. Meanwhile, the XRD patterns of perovskites with different post-treatment processes shown in Figure 1c are nearly identical, with no obvious 2D perovskite phase observed. These results demonstrated that the post-treatment strategy did not alter the crystallization and morphology of WBG perovskites.
The binding state after various post-treatments was analyzed using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), the shifts in the Pb 4f peaks confirmed the existence of passivator molecules and the interaction with the $\mathrm{Pb}$ atom on the surface. The Pb 4f XPS spectrum of the control film contains two main peaks at 137.66 and $142.50\mathrm{eV},$ corresponding to the Pb 4f 7/2 and 5/2 orbitals, respectively. The Pb 4f peaks in the $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ -treated film shift toward higher binding energy by $0.29\mathrm{eV},$ while the 4F-PEACl and SIE passivated films shift with 0.06 and $0.10\mathrm{eV_{\mathrm{:}}}$ respectively. These shifts indicate the existence of the passivator molecules on the surface of passivated films. Notably, the binding energies shift of $\mathrm{Pb}4\mathrm{f}7/2$ and 5/2 orbitals in SIE samples was intermediate between $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ and 4FPEACl treated samples. The signal of F 1s obviously appears after deposition 4F-PEACl, which clearly means the existence of 4F-PEACl both on the 4F-PEACl and SIE perovskite surface. Furthermore, the time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry of the SIE sample is shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information). It can be seen that the 4F-PEA molecules are covered only on the surface of the perovskite film, while the EDA molecular can gradient penetrate into the perovskite film.
Time-resolved PL (TRPL) was conducted to analyze nonradiative recombination and charge extraction for the perovskite $\mathrm{^{\prime}C_{60}}$ stack (Figure 1e). As shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information), the effective decay time increased drastically for SIE samples with $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ $(234~\mathrm{ns})$ than control samples (86 ns), indicating mitigated nonradiative recombination. Surface recombination velocity (SRV) at the perovskite $\mathrm{^{\prime}C_{60}}$ interface (calculation method provided in Note S1, Supporting Information) was also evaluated,[18,19] revealing SRV values of 799, 488, 710, and $251\mathrm{{cm\s^{-1}}}$ for control, $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ , 4F-PEACl, and SIE samples, respectively (Table S1, Supporting Information). We found that 4FPEACl exhibited SRV similar to control samples, however, depositing 4F-PEACl on $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ -treated samples effectively reduced SRV at the perovskite $^{\prime}{\mathsf{C}}_{60}$ interface. Furthermore, SIE samples displayed substantially lower SRV than $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ samples, suggesting that the introduction of a dipole insert layer was pivotal for the enhanced quality of the WBG perovskite $'C_{60}$ stack. Moreover, Table S1 (Supporting Information) shows bi-exponential decays with a fast $(\tau_{1})$ and a slow $(\tau_{2})$ component. In general, the slow decay component $(\tau_{2})$ is attributed to the radiative recombination, whereas the fast decay component $(\tau_{1})$ originates from charge carriers extraction at the interface.[20] The SIE possesses the lowest $\tau_{1}$ and the longest $\tau_{2}$ means the optimized charge extraction properties and radiative recombination among all samples. In conclusion, the SIE strategy can effectively mitigate nonradiative recombination and meanwhile, increase charge extraction.
The statistical deviation of the photovoltaic parameters for the optimized WBG perovskite solar cells with different posttreatment are summarized in Figure 2a. Compared with the control group, the incorporation of $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ resulted in significant enhancements, raising $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ by $\approx40~\mathrm{mV},$ FF by $5\%$ , and $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ value by $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . Only 4F-PEACl also resulted in improvements in optoelectronic properties than control samples, while the improvements were less than in $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ -treated samples. Figure 2b and Figure S5 (Supporting Information) shows the $J{-}V$ curves and the steady power output (SPO) of the optimum devices with different post-treatment process. The devices treated with SIE exhibited even further performance improvement, with a substantial $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ boost of $\approx70~\mathrm{mV},$ resulting in champion devices with a PCE of $21.8\%$ . The champion devices exhibited a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.262\mathrm{V},$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $20.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and FF of $82.7\%$ while maintaining negligible hysteresis. The corresponding external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra (Figure 2c) were consistent with the $J{-}V$ curves, further validating the enhanced device performance. By the way, the mixture of two passivators for single-step surface treatment or change in the sequence of two passivators treatment will not reach the PCE of the SIE process with $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ first, followed with 4F-PEACl as shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information).
Furthermore, we compared the champion $J{-}V$ curves of SIEbased devices (active area of $1.044\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) under illumination with and without an optical mask (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The unshaded device achieved a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.275\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , reaching $91.6\%$ of the SQ limit, which is among the highest reported values for WBG perovskites with a bandgap of $1.67\mathrm{eV.}$ In addition, we analyzed the FF loss in perovskite devices with an active area of $1.044~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (Figure 2d). The measured 4-wire split $J{-}V$ curve (Figure S8, Supporting Information), assuming the $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ was 0- ohm cm, yielded results comparable to the pseudo-J–V curve,[21] highlighting that the primary FF loss in $1.044~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device originated from the $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ in the circuit. Notably, the $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ value mainly depends on the square resistance of the ITO, indicating the crucial role of the ITO substrate resistance in FF loss.
To further demonstrate the $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ improvement, we quantified the interfacial non-radiative recombination loss at the perovskite $\ensuremath{\langle\mathbf{C}_{60}\rangle}$ interface using quasi-Fermi level splitting (QFLS).[22] The absolute PL photoluminescence spectra of perovskite films on glass/ITO/NiO/SAM without (Figure 3a) and with $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{60}}$ (Figure 3b) were measured, and the corresponding QFLS was shown in Figure 3c. Without $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ deposition, the QFLS values are 1298, 1295, 1290, and $1298~\mathrm{mV}$ for control, $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ , 4F-PEACl, and SIE samples, respectively. The nearly unchanged QFLS indicates that all post-treatment could not effectively passivate defects in the perovskite film and surfaces. However, the introduction of the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ layer triggered significant interfacial non-radiative recombination in the control stack, evidenced by the remarkably reduced PL intensity with QFLS loss of $75~\mathrm{mV}.$ The deposition of 4F-PEACl and $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ effectively suppressed non-radiative recombination, while the sequential combination of SIE almost entirely eliminated it, corroborated by unchanged QFLS compared to bare perovskite. As shown in Figure 3d, the significant suppression of non-radiative recombination loss was further supported by the highest external quantum efficiency (EL) observed in the perovskite single-junction solar cells with SIE. The ELEQE closely matched QFLS calculated from PLQY, indicating the negligible band offset and non-radiative recombination between the perovskite and charge transport layer.[23]
The suppression of interfacial non-radiative loss enables the high $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of devices, while for FF, transport properties also play a crucial role. We further demonstrated that SIE not only effectively suppressed the interfacial non-radiative loss but also facilitated the charge transport at the perovskite $\mathrm{\DeltaC}_{60}$ interface. Analysis of pseudo- $J V_{:}$ , non-radiative loss, and transport loss of FF for perovskite solar cells with different post-treatments was performed (Figure 3e), utilizing intensity-dependent $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Figure 3f summarizes a comparison of the different contributions to FF losses for the sample. The control samples enabled a “pseudo-FF” (pFF) value of $85.6\%$ , $5.4\%$ of the FF loss due to non-radiative recombination, and $9.7\%$ due to transport loss occurring in control samples. Devices treated with $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ and 4F-PEACl showed lower non-radiative loss and transport loss of FF compared to the control device. When the sequential deposition of SIE was adopted, the device showed the highest FF with the lowest non-radiative loss $(3.3\%)$ and transport loss $(5.0\%)$ . These results demonstrated the efficacy of our approach in simultaneously reducing the non-radiative and transport loss. In addition, the SIE process is also suitable for perovskite with different bandgap and different cation combinations as shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information).
We fabricated monolithic perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells using a double-textured Si bottom cell with a sub-micrometer random pyramid structure (Figure 4a). The double-textured side enhanced the near-infrared absorption, whereas the submicrometer enabled the deposition of spin-coated perovskite. The typical silicon solar cells exhibited a PCE of $21.7\%$ with a
$J_{S C}$ of $40.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $0.700~\mathrm{V},$ and FF of $76.6\%$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Cross-sectional SEM images of the front (top) and backside (bottom) of tandem solar cells are shown in Figure 4b. It is evident that the perovskites fully covered the textured silicon without pinhole formation, with the maximum thickness of the perovskite layer exceeding $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Figure 4c compares the PCE histogram of ${\approx}30$ control and SIE devices processed among several batches. The statistical mean PCE of control devices was ${\approx}26.5\%$ , whereas the sequential surface-treated devices achieved $\approx28.5\%$ .
The champion SIE-based device achieved a remarkable reverse-scan PCE of up to $29.8\%$ with a $J_{S C}$ of $20.1\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.941\mathrm{~V},$ and FF of $76.6\%$ (Figure 4d), whereas the control device showed the highest PCE of $27.7\%$ . The EQE of tandem devices (Figure 4e) exhibited a matching current density of $20.0\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ , in great agreement with the $J{-}V$ curve. The steady power output of champion tandem devices reached $29.6\%$ (Figure 4f), closely consistent with the reverse-scan result. Table 1 shows the detailed PV parameters of the control and SIE WBG single-junction and tandem PSCs. The PCE improvement in tandem structure was mainly attributed to enhancements in $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ and FF in the WBG perovskite subcell as discussed above (Figure S12, Supporting Information). To ensure the credibility of our findings, a tandem cell was submitted to an accredited independent PV calibration laboratory (JET) for independent certification, confirming a certified quasisteady-state PCE of $29.0\%$ (Figure S13, Supporting Information). This validation shows the promising potential of our approach in enhancing the efficiency of perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we focused on addressing the crucial issue of interfacial recombination loss in WBG perovskites, which hampers their overall efficiency. To tackle this challenge, we devised a two-step sequential post-treatment strategy involving $\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}$ and 4F-PEACl. This innovative approach effectively reduces the conduction band offset and enhances the positive dipole at the perovskite $/\mathrm{C}_{60}$ interface. By successfully suppressing nonradiative charge recombination and enhancing charge extraction, we substantially improved the $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ and FF of WBG perovskite solar cells. The champion WBG perovskite solar cell (1.67 eV) achieved a PCE of $21.8\%$ with an impressive $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.262~\mathrm{V}.$
Furthermore, the efficiency of the perovskite-silicon monolithic tandem cell on double-textured silicon was remarkably boosted to $29.6\%$ , with a certified PCE of $29.0\%$ . These advancements highlight the potential of our post-treatment approach in enhancing the performance of WBG perovskite solar cells and enabling the realization of highly efficient perovskite-silicon photovoltaic technology.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: All materials were used as received without further purification. FAI, MABr, and 4-Fluoro-Phenethylammonium chloride (4F-PEACl) were purchased from GreatCell Solar Materials. $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(99.99\%)$ , $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ $(99.99\%)$ , CsI $(99.9\%)$ , 2PACz ([2-(9H-carbazol-9- yl)ethyl]phosphonic acid) $(>98.0\%)$ and MeO-2PACz ([2-(3,6-dimethoxy9H-carbazol-9-yl)ethyl]phosphonic acid) $(>98.0\%)$ were purchased from Tokyo Chemical Industry (TCI). DMF $(99.8\%)$ , DMSO $(99.9\%)$ , anisole $(99.7\%)$ , isopropanol $(19\mathsf{A},99.8\%)$ and were purchased from SigmaAldrich. $\mathsf{E D A l}_{2}$ (ethylenediamine diiodide) was purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology. The ${\sf C}_{60}$ was purchased from Nano-C. NiO target, IZO target, and ${\mathsf{M g F}}_{2}$ $(99.999\%)$ were purchased from ZhongNuo Advanced Material Technology Co., Ltd. Tetrakis (dimethylamino) tin(iv) $(99.9999\%)$ ) was purchased from Nanjing Ai Mou Yuan Scientific Equipment.
Silicon Bottom Solar Cell Fabrication: Silicon heterojunction bottom cells were fabricated on n-type double-textured silicon wafers (CZ, 4 inches, $\protect1-5\ \Omega$ resistivity, $280~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ thickness). The intrinsic, n-type, and ptype hydrogenated amorphous silicon layers were deposited on both sides of the wafer using plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition with a process temperature of $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The back contact of the silicon cells was fabricated by stacking sputtering ITO $(80\mathsf{n m})$ and then thermal evaporated $\mathsf{A g}$ $(750\mathsf{n m})$ . The front $20\mathsf{n m}$ ITO recombination layer was sputtered through an aligned mask $(75~\mathrm{mm}\times10~\mathrm{mm})$ ).
Single Junction Perovskite Solar Cells Fabrication: The ITO glass was ultrasonically cleaned with water, acetone, and IPA for 15 min. After UVOzone treatment for 15 min, $20~\mathsf{n m}$ NiO film was sputtered on the substrate. The $0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\ L^{-1}$ Self-assembled molecules (SAM) solutions using mixture $2{\mathsf{P A C}}z$ : MeO-2PACz (1:3 molar ratio) in IPA were mixed were spin-coated on the NiO film at 4000 r.p.m. for $20~\mathsf{s}$ followed by annealing at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 5 min. $\mathsf{C s O.O5F A O.8M A O.75P b}(\mathsf{I0.75B r O.25})3$ perovskite precursor solution with a concentration of $\ensuremath{\mathsf{1.7}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{m}}$ was prepared by dissolving FAI, MABr, $\mathsf{C s l}$ , PbI2, and PbBr2 in mixed DMF and DMSO solvents (volume ratio $=4:7$ ). $\mathsf{F A}_{0.8}\mathsf{C s}_{0.2}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.6}\mathsf{B r}_{0.4})_{3}$ , $\mathsf{F A}_{0.8}\mathsf{C s}_{0.2}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.8}\mathsf{B r}_{0.2})_{3}$ , and $\mathsf{F A}_{0.8}\mathsf{C s}_{0.2}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.9}\mathsf{B r}_{0.7})_{3}$ perovskite precursor solutions with a concentration of $1.7~\mathsf{M}$ was prepared by dissolving FAI, CsI, $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ in mixed DMF and DMSO solvents (volume ratio $=4:7$ ). The perovskite precursor was spin-coated on SAM at 2000 rpm for $50\ \mathrm{~s~}$ , then followed with 6000 rpm for $\rceil5\textrm{s}$ . An anisole of $300~\upmu\upmu\up$ was dropped on the substrate 12 s before the end of the perovskite spin-coating process. Then, the samples were annealed at ${100}~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . For the perovskite surface treatment, $\mathsf{E D A l}_{2}$ $(1\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}})$ in mixed IPA: Toluene (1:1 volume ratio) and 4F-PEACl $(1\ \mathsf{m g}\ \mathsf{m L^{-1}})$ in IPA was prepared with a spin speed of 5 krpm for $20~\mathsf{s}$ , the $50~\upmu\up L$ of treatment solution was dropped on the perovskite film when the spin speed reach maximum rotational speed. The perovskite films require annealing at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $5\mathrm{\min}$ in each step. $75\ensuremath{\mathrm{~nm~}}\mathsf C_{60}$ was deposited by thermal evaporation and $75\ \mathsf{n m}\ \mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ was deposited by atomic layer deposition at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Finally, a $200\ \mathsf{n m\ A g}$ back electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation.
Perovskite/Silicon Monolithic Tandem Solar Cells Fabrication: Before the fabrication of perovskite top cells, the 4-inch silicon wafers were laser cut to $2.5\times2.5$ cm substrates. Then the silicon substrates were annealed at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $10\min$ to recover the sputtering damage. The HTL, perovskite, interfacial layer, ${\sf C}_{60}$ , and $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ were the same as single-junction perovskite solar cells. After the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ deposition, $80\ \mathsf{n m}$ of IZO was sputtered onto $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ through a shadow mask. $200\mathsf{n m}\mathsf{A g}$ finger was fabricated by thermal evaporation with a shadow mask. Finally, ${\mathsf{M g F}}_{2}$ with a thickness of $100\mathsf{n m}$ was deposited by electron beam evaporation.
Solar Cells Characterization: The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ measurement of single-junction solar cells was carried out using a Kithley 4200 source meter with a Xenon lamp solar simulator with AM 1.5G spectrum $(700~\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ in a nitrogen-filled glovebox. For tandem solar cells, the $J{-}V$ measurement was carried out under a two-lamp solar simulator with a light intensity of $\mathsf{l o o\ m w\ c m}^{-2}$ , calibrated by a standard silicon cell (NREL). The active area defined by the aperture shade mask of singlejunction and tandem solar cells was 0.049 and $\mathsf{1.044~c m^{2}}$ , respectively. EQE spectra were measured using a QE system (EnliTech) with monochromatic light focused on the active area of the device. For tandems, lightemitting diodes with emission peaks at 850 and $460\mathsf{n m}$ were used as bias illumination to measure the top and bottom subcells.
Other Characterization: XRD patterns were measured using a Bruker D8 AVANTAGE Diffractometer with Cu K𝛼 $(\lambda=1.54\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ radiation. XPS analysis was performed using the ThermoScientific Al K-Alpha XPS system with energy steps of 0.1 eV. SEM images were obtained using a TESCAN microscope. Optical absorption of the perovskite films was measured using a Lambda 950 UV/Vis spectrophotometer. UPS with an excitation energy of $21.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and an energy resolution of $50\mathrm{\meV}$ was performed to obtain the secondary electron cutoff and the valence band onset. The absolute PL measurement was performed with a $405\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser with 1-sun equivalent intensity and an Ocean Optics CCD camera. TRPL was measured using a Horiba Fluorolog-3 time-correlated single photon counting system with an excitation $485~\mathsf{n m}$ layer. The effective lifetime of perovskite films was calculated by fitting the biexponential components of PL decay curves.
